// case: float-literal-truncated-to-int
// what: `print(1.5)` prints 1 on the released canonical. A float in Orbit is
//      not a value: the literal is parsed, the register holds 2.5 for `2.5`
//      (both halves are read by the lexer), and then every step from the
//      register to the argument to `print` is a `uintptr_t` while `print`
//      emits `%lld`. The integer part is all that survives, so the fraction is
//      gone with no diagnostic anywhere. Measured on the canonical:
//
//        print(1.5)      -> 1
//        print(0.5)      -> 0
//        print(-2.25)    -> -2      (toward zero, not toward -infinity)
//        print(3.14159)  -> 3
//        print(100.0)    -> 100     (agrees by accident, not by working)
//
//      Arithmetic on floats agrees only where the truncation is invisible:
//      `print(1.5 + 1.5)` prints 3 because both halves round the same way. So
//      43 of the differential fuzzer's 44 float cases disagree and the one that
//      does not is the exponent form, which is refused.
//
//      TWO HALVES, TWO CASES, AND THE SPLIT MOVES. This one is the literal: the
//      value is already wrong before it reaches a call, and formatting a
//      register the front end typed as a float through orbit_float_to_string
//      fixes all three measurements above. That fix is in the core tree and
//      NOT in the canonical, so this case is correct against the artifact every
//      gate in CI measures and WILL FIRE at the promote -- which is a ratchet
//      working, not a ratchet broken. When it fires, all three of these
//      measurements are right and this program belongs in tests/suite/ with the
//      right values as the expectation.
//
//      What no half fixes is the transport, and n35 (the core agent's case)
//      pins that: a parameter arrives as `(orbit_float)(uintptr_t)x`, so
//      `fn twice(x: float) -> float { return x + x }` applied to 1.5 gives 2.
//      Giving the transport room for a double is a language-scale change --
//      orbit_int is 32 bits and a register is one word -- which is why this is
//      pinned and not fixed. FINDINGS F-0025.
// known-defect: F-0025
// wrong-value: 1\n0\n-2
fn main() -> int {
    print(1.5)
    print(0.5)
    print(-2.25)
    return 0
}
